### Description
When decoding a hex-encoded string of odd length using `boost::algorithm::unhex`, the function attempts to read two characters at a time. On the final iteration, it accesses out of bounds of the input string and throws `std::out_of_range` instead of the documented domain exception `boost::algorithm::hex_decode_error` (with error code `not_enough_input`).

### Steps to Reproduce
1. Compile the following C++ snippet:
```cpp
#include <boost/algorithm/hex.hpp>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string input = "48656c6c6"; // 9 characters (odd length)
    std::string output;
    try {
        boost::algorithm::unhex(input, std::back_inserter(output));
        std::cout << "Decoded: " << output << std::endl;
    } catch (const boost::algorithm::hex_decode_error& e) {
        std::cout << "Caught expected hex_decode_error: " << e.what() << std::endl;
        return 0;
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cerr << "Unexpected exception thrown: " << e.what() << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
2. Run the executable with odd-length hex input.

### Expected Behavior
The function should validate the input length or catch boundary conditions and throw `boost::algorithm::hex_decode_error` with `boost::algorithm::not_enough_input`.

### Actual Behavior
The program terminates with an unhandled `std::out_of_range` exception (or fails to catch it under `hex_decode_error`).
